Task: 
We present a novel approach to address the challenge of satisfying group cardinality constraints in SQL queries by refining query predicates based on provenance information and algebraic expressions for constraint testing. Our solution is motivated by fairness-aware data mining, which has gained significant attention due to its importance in various domains such as employment, housing, lending, and college admissions. In this paper, we propose a novel approach that considers multiple sensitive attributes (e.g., gender or ethnicity) simultaneously while preserving the original query's properties as much as possible. We introduce Possible Value Lists(PVL), an efficient data structure for representing refinements based on provenance expressions, and present an algorithm to efficiently generate minimal refinements using PVL. Our experimental results demonstrate that our approach outperforms existing approaches in terms of efficiency and effectiveness.

Task: 

In the context of fairness-aware query processing, we introduce a new problem called Query Refinement Problem (QRP), which involves finding minimal refinements to an initial SQL query given a dataset and constraints on the cardinality of the result set. We propose a provenance model that annotates tuples with relevant predicate information and translates cardinality constraints into algebraic expressions for constraint testing, following Green et al.[13]. Additionally, we introduce Possible Value Lists (PVL), an efficient data structure encompassing all possible predicate values, and employ an efficient searching algorithm with pruning techniques to identify minimal refinements. Our solution addresses the challenges of time-consuming execution and computationally expensive exhaustive analysis by adopting a provenance model that annotates tuples with relevant predicate information and translating cardinality constraints into algebraic expressions for constraint testing, following Green et al.[13]. Additionally, we introduce Possible Value Lists (PVL), an efficient data structure encompassing all possible predicate values, and employ an efficient searching algorithm with pruning techniques to identify minimal refinements. Our approach is applicable in many other contexts beyond fairness-aware query processing.

In the running example of a job application process, we demonstrate how our solution can be applied to satisfy group cardinality constraints while preserving the original selection process as much as possible. We also discuss alternative approaches and their limitations compared to our work. In Section 6, we experimentally evaluate our solution with multiple datasets, queries, and constraints.

